---
title: Banana bread
description: A test recipe for the new recipes section — a moist, not-too-sweet banana bread with a crackly top. Good for using up the bananas that went too far.
publishedAt: 2026-11-18
draft: false
servings: 10 slices
prepTime: 15 minutes
cookTime: 55 minutes
ingredients:
  - component: ''
    items:
      - 3 very ripe bananas, mashed (about 1 cup)
      - 115 g unsalted butter, melted and cooled
      - 100 g caster sugar
      - 50 g brown sugar, lightly packed
      - 2 large eggs, at room temperature
      - 1 tsp vanilla extract
      - 200 g plain flour
      - 1 tsp baking soda
      - ½ tsp fine sea salt
      - ½ tsp ground cinnamon
      - 60 g walnuts, roughly chopped (optional)
---

## Method

1. Heat the oven to 175°C fan-forced (195°C conventional). Grease a 22 x 12 cm
   loaf tin and line it with baking paper, leaving an overhang on the long sides.
2. Mash the bananas in a large bowl until mostly smooth. A few lumps are fine
   and keep the crumb interesting.
3. Whisk in the melted butter, caster sugar, brown sugar, eggs, and vanilla
   until everything is combined and slightly glossy.
4. In a separate bowl, whisk the flour, baking soda, salt, and cinnamon.
5. Fold the dry ingredients into the wet with a spatula until just combined.
   Stop as soon as there are no dry streaks; overmixing makes the loaf tough.
   Fold through the walnuts.
6. Scrape the batter into the tin and smooth the top. Bake for 50–60 minutes,
   until a skewer inserted in the centre comes out with a few moist crumbs. If
   the top is browning too quickly after 40 minutes, tent it loosely with foil.
7. Cool in the tin for 10 minutes, then lift it out and finish cooling on a
   wire rack before slicing.

## Notes

- The riper the bananas, the sweeter the bread. Black-spotted skins are ideal.
- For muffins, use the same batter and bake for 20–25 minutes.
- The loaf keeps for about 3 days in an airtight container, or freezes well for
  up to 3 months.
